import sys
p = sys.argv[1]
lines = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read().split('\n')
idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith('| 635 |')]
assert len(idx) == 1
lines.insert(idx[0] + 1, "| 636 | 2026-10-23 | **Intervals rebuilt in the Keys and staff style (her 18:10 call: finish Intervals first, then Chords).** Nine new lessons on KqEngine (sc/iv.jsx, spliced by sc/patch658.py), each whole picture, one by one, your turn; keyboard first, the staff on request (and one staff lesson). Home shows four groups, each with one Questions button that opens its level of the old intervals drill: (1) The number: Count the letters (lower note is 1, counting down too), Intervals on the staff (every line and space counts; odd stays, even swaps); level 1. (2) Major, minor and perfect: Count the half steps (hop arrows; C-E 4 = major 3rd, D-F 3 = minor 3rd), Above the key note (C, G, D, F major: 2/3/6/7 major, 4/5/8 perfect), One half step smaller (major to minor; 4ths, 5ths, octaves stay perfect); level 3. (3) Hearing: Melodic and harmonic (a harmonic 2nd written side by side), Hear the size (m3/M3, P4/P5, P8); level 7. (4) Upside down and the tritone: inversion (numbers add to 9, major to minor, perfect stays), tritone (6 half steps; augmented 4th or diminished 5th by the letters); level 8. The ten old Toolbox tools stay in the Toolbox. Also: kqKb passes hop arrows; a concept's first group opens on arrival; lesson titles use lining figures. **For her review (music facts and Korean terms, not from a checked source):** every half-step count above; the major/perfect rule above the key note; the harmonic 2nd offset; 'played together an octave sounds almost like one note'; inversion rules; the tritone names; Korean terms 선율 음정/화성 음정, 장음정/단음정/완전음정, 증4도/감5도, 트라이톤 and the short labels 장2, 완4 on the keyboard. She confirmed (18:09) that the right-hand fingerings of B flat, E flat and A flat major starting on finger 2 are correct. |")
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(lines)); print('ok')
